{"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"SplayTree"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"Element"},{"kind":"text","text":"> "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"where"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Element"},{"kind":"text","text":" : "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:SL","text":"Comparable"}],"platforms":["Linux"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A splay tree keeps no balance information. Any sequence of "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"m","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" insertions, removals and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"accesses on "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"n"}]},{"type":"text","text":" elements costs O(("},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"m"}]},{"type":"text","text":" + "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"n","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":") log "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"n"}]},{"type":"text","text":") in all, so each takes O(log "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"n","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":")"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"amortized, and recently or frequently accessed elements stay near the root: accessing the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"elements in ascending order costs O("},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"n"}]},{"type":"text","text":") in all. Reads ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"contains(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":", the bounds, the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"subscripts and iteration) leave the tree as it is, so they keep the value semantics and the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"API of the other sorted sets, and take time proportional to the element’s depth, which a run"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of ascending insertions can make "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"n","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Use "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/BinarySearchTrees\/documentation\/BinarySearchTrees\/SplayTree\/access(_:)","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" to look an element up and splay it."}]},{"code":["var recent: SplayTree = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]","recent.access(40)             \/\/ 40, now at the root","recent.contains(25)           \/\/ false, and the tree is unchanged","let parts = recent.split(at: 30)","Array(parts.lower)            \/\/ [10, 20]","Array(parts.upper)            \/\/ [30, 40, 50]"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The elements are a random-access collection in ascending order, indexed by rank like an"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"array: "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tree[0]"},{"type":"text","text":" is the smallest element. The vertices live in flat arrays with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int32"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"links, so the tree holds fewer than 2³¹ − 1 elements, and copying it copies a few arrays only"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"when one copy is mutated."}]}],"kind":"content"}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/BinarySearchTrees\/documentation\/BinarySearchTrees\/SplayTree","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"sections":[],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A sorted set kept in a splay tree: a binary search tree that rotates each element it"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"accesses, inserts or removes up to the root (Sleator and Tarjan, 1985)."}],"var